Article 3
This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and children in the region;
b. prevent the occurrence of actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;
c. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
d. protect and provide a sense of security for victims of violence;
e. provide services to victims of violence; and
f. carry out comprehensive recovery for victims of violence.

Article 47
(1) Regional Government in efforts to fulfill the rights of Children is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of Children's rights;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, group, gender, ethnic origin, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing Children's rights;
d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children by taking into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the Child;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the fulfillment of Children's rights;
f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the framework of implementing the fulfillment of Children's rights;
h. conducting cooperation with private parties and/or NGOs in the implementation of Child Protection;
(2) The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts for Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by OPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 59
Regional Governments are obligated to provide Social Welfare for:
a. Children in conflict with the law;
b. Child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of human trafficking;
c. Children who do not have parents and are cared for by parties or families who are unable to provide;
d. Child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; and
e. Children with disabilities.

Article 55
(1) Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 54 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status more optimally.
(2) Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or education to the community regarding healthy lifestyle patterns which include:
a. the importance of exclusive breast milk for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food; and
e. the dangers of narcotics and smoking.
(3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government may carry out other activities in the context of health administration.

Article 56
(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 54 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.
(2) The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by: a. obligating mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to 6 (six) months of age;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers; c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting periodic health examinations;
f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water at every school; and g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms at government agencies or business entities.

